.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
.TH HEAPLINE-REPORT 1 @HEAPLINE_MAN_DATE@ "heapline @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
heapline-report \- summarize the heap usage records of heapline
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B heapline-report
.RB [ \-\-top
.IR N " | " \fB\-\-all\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-csv
.IR FILE ]
.IR RECORD_OR_DIR ...
.br
.B heapline-report
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B heapline-report
reads the records that the heapline library writes, given as files or found
as
.I *.csv
files in directories, and prints a summary of the job: the highest peak heap,
its rank and when it was reached, the median, minimum and imbalance of the
peaks; per node, the sum of the peak resident set sizes
.RB ( VmHWM )
of its ranks, the figure to compare with the
.B \-\-mem
request of a Slurm job; and the ranks with the highest peaks (see
.BR \-\-top ).
.PP
Files that cannot be read, that are not version 1 records, or that hold
missing or bad values are reported on standard error and skipped.
Gaps in the rank numbers are reported as missing ranks, two files that hold
the same rank are named, and the jobs are listed when the records come from
several Slurm jobs.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-top " N"
List the
.I N
ranks with the highest heap peak (10 by default).
.TP
.B \-\-all
List every rank.
.TP
.BI \-\-csv " FILE"
Also write all records, sorted by rank, to
.IR FILE .
Write it outside the records directory: every
.I .csv
file of that directory is read as a record.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The summary was printed.
.TP
.B 1
No record or directory is given, no record is valid, an option is wrong, or
.I FILE
cannot be written.
.SH RECORDING
heapline is a shared library loaded with
.BR LD_PRELOAD .
The program needs no change and no rebuild.
At exit, a one-line summary goes to standard error.
Set
.B HEAPLINE_OUTPUT
to also write a record of the process.
In its value,
.B %p
is replaced with the process id,
.B %r
with the MPI rank and
.B %h
with the host name.
The directory must exist: when the file cannot be opened, the record goes to
standard error.
.PP
The rank is read from
.BR OMPI_COMM_WORLD_RANK ,
.BR PMIX_RANK ,
.BR PMI_RANK ,
.B MV2_COMM_WORLD_RANK
or
.BR SLURM_PROCID ,
in this order, and is \-1 outside MPI.
.SH EXAMPLES
One program, with the library installed in the default search path:
.PP
.RS
.EX
LD_PRELOAD=libheapline.so HEAPLINE_OUTPUT=run.csv ./app
heapline\-report run.csv
.EE
.RE
.PP
An MPI job, one record per rank:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mkdir records
mpirun \-n 16 env LD_PRELOAD=libheapline.so \e
    HEAPLINE_OUTPUT=$PWD/records/heapline.%h.%r.csv ./app
heapline\-report records
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
On a cluster, the library must be installed on every node that runs the
program.
Where it is missing, the dynamic linker prints that it cannot be preloaded,
ignores it, and the job runs without writing any record.
Otherwise, give
.B LD_PRELOAD
the absolute path of the library on a shared file system.
.PP
A process killed by a signal writes no record.
Statically linked programs, and programs that use another allocator such as
jemalloc or tcmalloc, are not seen.
.SH SEE ALSO
The documentation, with the format of the records and the meaning of each
figure, is at https://ethan\-puyaubreau.github.io/heapline/.
